# Documentation Conventions

## Do NOT document uncommitted / working-tree state

Marco handles commits himself at the end of the day. The agent must not
treat uncommitted changes as something to be tracked, summarized, or
handed off in persistent documentation.

This rule applies to:

- `AWSMigration/HANDOFF.md` and any other `HANDOFF.md` / handoff-style docs
- `.cursor/rules/project/docs/known-issues.mdc`
- Any other living-doc file under `.cursor/rules/project/docs/`

Specifically, do NOT:

- Add a "What remains uncommitted" / "Still uncommitted in the working
  tree" / "Pending commit" section to a handoff or known-issues entry.
- List files currently `modified` per `git status` as part of the
  documented project state.
- Suggest commit messages inside the doc body as a permanent artifact
  (suggesting one in chat for Marco to use is fine -- writing it into
  the markdown is not).
- Describe the working tree as a deliverable. Document only what has
  been committed, what is operationally true on the relevant host, or
  what is decided as deferred work.

Why: these docs are read days or weeks later by an agent (or by Marco)
to understand the *committed, durable* state of the project. Pointing
at the transient working tree at the moment a doc was written makes
the doc obsolete the moment Marco commits, and creates churn in
follow-up sessions trying to reconcile "still uncommitted as of when
this doc was last updated" against the current `git status`.

## What IS appropriate in these docs

- The committed state of code, infra, and decisions.
- Operationally-verified facts about hosts (e.g. sha256 of a file on
  the production VM, systemd unit status, package versions installed).
- Deferred work items that are deliberately not being done now, with
  the reason and the conditions under which they would be revisited.
- Historical decisions and their rationale, so the next agent does
  not re-litigate them.

## If the agent notices uncommitted changes mid-session

- Mention them in chat to Marco, with a suggested commit message if
  helpful.
- Do not write them into a handoff or known-issues entry.
- Do not stage or commit them without explicit confirmation (per
  `generic/git-conventions.mdc`).
